.Dd November 18, 2026
.Dt SETTINGSHELPER 8
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm SettingsHelper
.Nd make root-only settings changes for the settings apps
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm /usr/libexec/SettingsHelper
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
makes the settings changes that need root, such as the Energy Saver
settings, the time zone, the date and time, and network time, for
NextBSD's settings apps.
It is started by
.Xr launchd 8
on demand as
.Ql org.nextbsd.SettingsHelper ,
serves the Mach service of that name, and exits once it has been idle for
30 seconds and holds no unexpired credential.
It is not meant to be run by hand.
.Pp
Each operation is a typed Mach call (no shell commands and no file paths)
and needs a right named
.Ql org.nextbsd.settings. Ns Ar area .
.Nm
answers only root and processes in the console user's session, as
.Xr loginwindow 8
records it in
.Pa /var/run/loginwindow .
.Ss Operations
.Bl -tag -width "SetNetworkTime"
.It Cm SetPMPreferences
one power source's Energy Saver settings
.Pq right Ql org.nextbsd.settings.energy .
.It Cm SetTimeZone
copies a zone from
.Pa /usr/share/zoneinfo
to
.Pa /etc/localtime ,
records its name in
.Pa /var/db/zoneinfo ,
as
.Xr tzsetup 8
does, and posts
.Ql com.apple.system.timezone
.Pq right Ql org.nextbsd.settings.datetime .
.It Cm SetDate
sets the clock with
.Xr settimeofday 2
and posts
.Ql com.apple.system.clock_set
.Pq right Ql org.nextbsd.settings.datetime .
.It Cm SetNetworkTime
turns
.Ql org.nextbsd.ntpd
on or off with
.Ql launchctl load -w
or
.Ql unload -w ,
and can replace the server and pool lines of
.Pa /etc/ntp.conf
.Pq right Ql org.nextbsd.settings.datetime .
.El
.Ss Rights
The rules have the shape of Darwin's authorization database.
Each right names a rule, or is one:
.Bl -tag -width "authenticate-session-owner"
.It Cm allow
anyone in the console session
.It Cm is-admin
the console user is in group
.Ql admin
.It Cm authenticate-admin
an administrator's name and password
.It Cm authenticate-session-owner
the console user's own password
.El
.Pp
A rule's
.Cm timeout
(default 300 seconds) says how long a successful authentication lasts for
the session; 0 makes it good for one change.
Passwords are checked with the PAM service
.Ql authorization
and are never stored.
An administrator whose account has no password authenticates with an
empty one, which the settings apps present as
.Dq Allow changes?
without a password field.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/Local/Library/Security/SettingsRights.plist" -compact
.It Pa /System/Library/Security/SettingsRights.plist
the rights and rules
.It Pa /Local/Library/Security/SettingsRights.plist
a site's rights and rules, which win over the system's
.It Pa /etc/pam.d/authorization
the PAM service passwords are checked with
.It Pa /System/Library/LaunchDaemons/org.nextbsd.SettingsHelper.plist
the launchd job
.It Pa /etc/localtime , /var/db/zoneinfo
the time zone, as SetTimeZone writes it
.It Pa /etc/ntp.conf
the time server, as SetNetworkTime writes it
.It Pa /var/log/SettingsHelper.log
its log: who was allowed what, and refusals
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pmset 1 ,
.Xr settimeofday 2 ,
.Xr launchd 8 ,
.Xr ntpd 8 ,
.Xr pam 8 ,
.Xr tzsetup 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in NextBSD 27.0.
